{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices (often by ~3–10 EUR), especially during the downtrend regime.",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: the model underestimates downside momentum and is slow to follow sharp selloffs; it keeps prices near the recent history level while the market continues falling.",
    "Insufficient mean-reversion to the downside: after a break lower, the model projects stabilization/rebound too early; realized path keeps drifting down for 2–4 weeks.",
    "Horizon effect: error grows with forecast horizon—near-term (days 1–5) is closer, but days ~10–30 show increasing overprediction and overly optimistic rebounds.",
    "Volatility underreaction on drops: large negative moves in the true series are not matched in amplitude; apply an extra downward adjustment when the last 5–10 days show accelerating declines.",
    "Spike/overshoot behavior: when the model forecasts late-horizon upticks (sometimes sharp), realized outcomes do not follow; dampen late-horizon upward swings (shrink toward a lower trend/level)."
  ]
}
